 "finding": "The two arms scale differently, and that is the result. The per-ray-wavelength arm is linear in the ray count wherever its own cost dominates -- exponent 0.981 at r^2 0.9983 above 10 k rays -- because it is O(rays) of host-side tuple construction done once per surface, which no chunk size amortizes. The size-1 arm is near-flat to 1 M rays: exponent 0.097 at r^2 0.4484, a poor power-law fit precisely because a flat cost is not a power law. What is left after the fix is per-call overhead, which is why the cost model below is affine rather than a scaling exponent.",
